+# Project Architecture
+
+## Information Sources
+
+This architecture is derived solely from `project.md`, `context.md`,
+`product-contract.md`, and `evidence-contract.md`. No external research was
+used. Technology choices below are reversible pilot assumptions made to turn
+the supplied Python API and browser UI constraint into an implementable plan.
+
+## Research Informing This Architecture
+
+No research reports or web findings were supplied. The four-week, two-engineer
+pilot favours a small deployable monolith over a distributed design.
+
+## Executive Summary
+
+Pulseboard will be a browser application backed by one Python API and one
+relational database. A transcript becomes candidate actions, then a facilitator
+must explicitly confirm each action's text, owner, and due date before the API
+can request one reminder from a provider selected through a small adapter.
+
+## Architectural Style
+
+### Selected Pattern
+
+- **Primary style**: Deployable modular monolith.
+- **Secondary patterns**: REST API, relational multi-tenancy, provider adapter
+  for reminder delivery.
+
+### Justification
+
+The pilot has two engineers, a four-week window, and no need for independent
+service scaling. Keeping extraction, review, authorization, and delivery in
+one codebase makes the human-confirmation boundary and the tenant boundary
+auditable.
+
+### Trade-offs
+
+- **Advantages**: Fast delivery, a single transaction boundary, and simple
+  runtime evidence.
+- **Disadvantages**: Reminder sending is synchronous unless a later queue is
+  added; a provider outage can delay delivery status.
+- **Mitigations**: Persist delivery attempts and status, expose retryable
+  failures, and keep the provider behind an interface.
+
+## System Architecture
+
+### High-Level Overview
+
+```text
+Browser UI
+  | upload transcript / review proposals / view delivery state
+  v
+Python REST API
+  |-- workspace authorization
+  |-- transcript and action workflow
+  |-- reminder-provider adapter
+  v
+PostgreSQL                         Configured reminder provider
+workspaces, memberships,           one delivery request per confirmed action
+transcripts, actions, attempts
+```
+
+### Component Breakdown
+
+| Component | Purpose | Technology | Scaling strategy |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Browser UI | Facilitator upload, review, and status workflow | TypeScript browser application | Static assets; add CDN only if needed |
+| API | Enforces workflow and workspace authorization | Python web API | Stateless process replicas if pilot load demands it |
+| Extractor | Produces proposed actions from transcript text | In-process application module | Isolate behind an interface before changing implementation |
+| Database | Durable workspace-scoped workflow records | PostgreSQL | Indexed workspace/action queries; managed backup |
+| Provider adapter | Sends a single approved reminder and records result | Python interface | Add providers without changing review rules |
+
+### Data Flow
+
+1. An authenticated facilitator uploads a transcript within a workspace.
+2. The API creates proposed action items associated with that workspace.
+3. The facilitator edits or confirms text, owner, and due date; only a complete
+   confirmation changes an item to schedulable.
+4. The API invokes the configured provider once for a confirmed action and
+   writes the resulting delivery status/attempt.
+5. Every request is workspace-scoped; a request outside the caller's workspace
+   returns no tenant data.
+
+## Technology Strategy
+
+### Technology Stack
+
+| Layer | Technology | Version | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Frontend | TypeScript browser UI | Current supported | Matches the supplied browser-UI constraint |
+| API layer | Python REST API | Current supported | Matches the supplied Python-API constraint |
+| Business logic | Python modules by workflow boundary | Current supported | Keeps review and delivery rules testable |
+| Data layer | PostgreSQL | Current supported | Supports transactional, workspace-scoped data |
+| Infrastructure | Container-ready single service deployment | To be selected | Appropriate for a small pilot |
+| Monitoring | Structured logs and application error reporting | To be selected | Required to diagnose pilot delivery failures |
+
+### Key Libraries & Frameworks
+
+Specific frameworks are deliberately deferred to E000 after the team confirms
+their existing Python and browser tooling. E000 must choose the API framework,
+UI build tool, migration tool, test runner, and error-reporting service without
+changing the architectural boundaries in this document.
+
+## Cross-Cutting Concerns
+
+### Security Architecture
+
+- **Authentication**: authenticated pilot users; implementation mechanism is
+  selected in E000.
+- **Authorization**: workspace membership is required on every transcript,
+  action, and delivery-status read or write.
+- **Data protection**: TLS in transit; database encryption and secret storage
+  supplied by the deployment environment.
+- **Threat mitigation**: tenant IDs come from the authenticated workspace
+  context, not a client-authoritative request field; authorization is tested
+  through real API request roles.
+
+### Performance Architecture
+
+- Optimize for interactive facilitator review rather than bulk processing.
+- Target a typical upload/review response under two seconds excluding any
+  explicitly surfaced extraction or provider delay.
+- Index records by workspace and action status; paginate action lists if needed.
+
+### Scalability Design
+
+Five pilot teams do not justify sharding or service decomposition. The API is
+stateless outside PostgreSQL so it can be replicated later. The extraction and
+delivery interfaces are the planned seams if asynchronous workers become
+necessary.
+
+### Reliability & Availability
+
+- **Pilot target**: availability during agreed pilot working hours; no numeric
+  SLA is supplied.
+- **Failure handling**: preserve failed delivery attempts and show status;
+  never claim delivery succeeded before the provider result is recorded.
+- **Recovery**: database backups and migration rollback are E000 requirements.
+
+## Data Architecture
+
+### Data Models
+
+```text
+Workspace 1---* Membership
+Workspace 1---* Transcript 1---* ProposedAction
+ProposedAction 1---* DeliveryAttempt
+```
+
+`ProposedAction` holds text, owner, due date, review state, and workspace ID.
+Only a confirmed action with all three required fields may create a
+`DeliveryAttempt`.
+
+### Storage Strategy
+
+| Data type | Storage solution | Backup strategy | Retention |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Workspace and workflow data | PostgreSQL | Automated database backup | Pilot duration; retention policy to be set before broader release |
+| Transcript text | PostgreSQL initially | Included in database backup | Pilot duration; deletion workflow is a follow-up requirement |
+| Delivery attempts | PostgreSQL | Included in database backup | Pilot duration |
+| Operational logs | Deployment logging service | Provider-managed | Minimize content; retention to be configured |
+
+### Data Governance
+
+- Treat transcripts and action details as confidential workspace data.
+- Collect no health or payment data under the supplied scope.
+- Record confirmation and delivery transitions for auditability.
+- Define pilot deletion/retention settings before onboarding teams.
+
+## Integration Architecture
+
+### External Services
+
+| Service | Purpose | Integration method | Fallback strategy |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Reminder provider (unselected) | Deliver one reminder | Provider adapter | Mark failure/pending and allow controlled retry; do not send autonomously |
+
+### API Design
+
+- **Style**: workspace-scoped REST endpoints.
+- **Versioning**: an internal pilot API version prefix if external clients are
+  introduced.
+- **Documentation**: OpenAPI generated from the selected Python framework.
+
+### Event Architecture
+
+No event bus is needed for the pilot. Workflow transitions are persisted in the
+database. A later outbox/worker can consume confirmed actions without changing
+the confirmation invariant.
+
+## Development & Deployment
+
+### Development Architecture
+
+- **Repository**: one repository for browser UI and Python API.
+- **Code organization**: separate UI, API, domain/workflow, persistence, and
+  provider-adapter modules.
+- **Branching**: short-lived branches merged through tested review.
+- **Environment**: local configuration uses environment variables; secrets are
+  never committed.
+
+### CI/CD Pipeline
+
+```text
+format/lint -> unit + API authorization tests -> browser build -> deploy pilot environment
+```
+
+### Deployment Architecture
+
+- Development and pilot environments are separated.
+- Containerization and hosting are selected in E000.
+- Deployments require a rollback path to the prior application version and
+  backward-compatible database migrations.
+
+## Operational Architecture
+
+### Monitoring & Observability
+
+- Measure proposal creation, confirmation completion, provider requests, and
+  delivery success/failure.
+- Use structured logs with workspace-safe identifiers; do not log transcript
+  content by default.
+- Alert the pilot operator on repeated provider failures.
+
+### Maintenance Windows
+
+Use rolling/short deployments where supported. Database migrations must be
+backward compatible during the pilot.
+
+## Architecture Decision Records
+
+### ADR-001: Modular monolith for the pilot
+- **Status**: Accepted for planning.
+- **Context**: Two engineers and four weeks need a small, auditable delivery
+  path.
+- **Decision**: One Python API and relational database with modular boundaries.
+- **Consequences**: Defer microservices and event infrastructure.
+
+### ADR-002: Provider adapter before provider selection
+- **Status**: Accepted for planning.
+- **Context**: The reminder provider is explicitly unselected.
+- **Decision**: Model delivery through an adapter and persist delivery state.
+- **Consequences**: Provider selection is an E002 implementation decision;
+  review confirmation remains provider-independent.
+
+## Risk Analysis
+
+| Risk | Probability | Impact | Mitigation |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Extraction proposes poor actions | Medium | High | Keep all proposals editable and require confirmation |
+| Tenant isolation defect | Low | High | Enforce workspace scope in the API and run real-role negative probes |
+| Provider is unavailable or unsuitable | Medium | Medium | Adapter, durable status, and controlled retry |
+| Four-week schedule compresses quality work | Medium | High | E000 establishes tests and deployment before feature work |
+
+## Evolution Strategy
+
+Review the architecture after the four-week pilot or if provider failures,
+extraction latency, or more than five teams expose a constraint. Split the
+extractor or delivery worker only when evidence shows the monolith boundary is
+the bottleneck.
+
+## Appendix
+
+### Glossary
+
+- **Proposed action**: an extracted candidate not yet authorized for delivery.
+- **Confirmed action**: a facilitator-approved action with text, owner, and due
+  date.
+- **Delivery attempt**: one recorded request to the configured reminder
+  provider.
+
+### References
+
+- `project.md`
+- `context.md`
+- `product-contract.md`
+- `evidence-contract.md`
